; pi4V3dFbClear.pi4 - stage 5a. THE GPU CLEARS THE REAL HDMI SURFACE.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHY A CLEAR AND NOT THE TRIANGLE, WHEN THE TRIANGLE ALREADY WORKS
; ======================================================================
;
;  pi4V3dTri.pi4 rasterises a triangle. It does it into a 64 x 64
;  buffer in DRAM, which is EXACTLY ONE TILE and one supertile, and
;  into memory that nothing scans out. Two things stand between that
;  and a picture on a monitor, and they are both plumbing:
;
;    1. MULTI-TILE. Every piece of tile arithmetic in v3d.pi4 - the
;       supertile sizing loop, the supertile sweep, the tile
;       allocation pool, the tile state buffer, the partial tiles at
;       the right and bottom edges - collapses to the identity at
;       64 x 64. NOTHING HAS EVER EXERCISED IT. 1920 x 1080 is 510
;       tiles and 135 supertiles.
;
;    2. A V3D PAGE TABLE THAT REACHES THE FRAMEBUFFER. Every address
;       the CLE, the PTB and the TLB touch is VIRTUAL - v3d_submit.c
;       :1054-1055 - and the framebuffer the firmware hands back sits
;       high in the low gigabyte. A table covering it is about a
;       megabyte, not the sixteen kilobytes every probe so far has
;       used.
;
;  THIS PROGRAM DOES BOTH OF THOSE AND NOTHING ELSE. No shader, no
;  vertex, no rasteriser: a flat colour. That is the whole point. If
;  the screen comes out wrong here, it is the plumbing; if it comes
;  out right here and wrong in pi4V3dFbTri.pi4, it is the geometry.
;  Skipping this step to save a bench run is what makes a black
;  screen undiagnosable.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE PASS CRITERION IS COUNTED PIXELS, PER TILE. NOT A PHOTOGRAPH.
; ======================================================================
;
;  The framebuffer is filled with poison from the CPU, the GPU is
;  asked to clear it, and then EVERY PIXEL IS READ BACK AND COUNTED -
;  two million of them - grouped BY TILE, so the answer is not "how
;  many pixels are right" but "how many of the 510 tiles are
;  completely right, and which is the first that is not".
;
;  A per-tile count is what makes this a multi-tile test rather than
;  a big single-tile test. A supertile sweep that emitted one
;  coordinate would leave 509 tiles full of poison and a global count
;  would still report "most pixels wrong", which names nothing. This
;  one names the tile.
;
;  THE COLOUR IS MULTI-BYTE ON PURPOSE. $FF0C145C - four distinct
;  bytes - so a channel swap, a byte-order fault or a 16-bit store
;  cannot pass. It is also what DisplayRGB(12, 20, 92) produces, so
;  the same word that the counter checks is the word display.pi4 says
;  means "deep navy", and the monitor is a second, independent
;  witness to the same number.
;
;  THERE ARE TWO GUARDS AND THEY GUARD DIFFERENT THINGS:
;
;    GUARD A, the page at #FB_GUARD, is DRAM this program owns and
;    DELIBERATELY DOES NOT MAP. Nothing the GPU does can reach it, so
;    a word that changed means an engine wrote through an address
;    this program never authorised.
;
;    GUARD B is the tail of the firmware's own framebuffer
;    ALLOCATION - the bytes between pitch*height and the size the
;    firmware reported. Part of it IS inside the last mapped page, so
;    it is reachable, which makes it the real overrun detector: a
;    store that runs one row long lands in it.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE MEMORY MAP
; ======================================================================
;
;  Identity-mapped through the V3D MMU, VA = PA, the same as every
;  V3D probe before it. The framebuffer is identity-mapped too, which
;  is the reason the table is a megabyte: the table is indexed by
;  virtual page number, so covering a VA of $3Exxxxxx costs four
;  bytes for every 4 KiB below it whether or not anything is mapped
;  there. Sparse in content, dense in extent.
;
;  A NON-IDENTITY WINDOW WOULD HAVE COST SIXTEEN KILOBYTES and was
;  refused. Mapping the framebuffer at some low VA works and is
;  cheaper, and it introduces a second address for one buffer - one
;  the CPU must never use and the GPU must always use. Every other
;  address in this file and in the five probes before it is the same
;  number on both sides. A megabyte of DRAM on a four-gigabyte board
;  is not worth the class of bug where the two are transposed.
;
; ======================================================================
;  RUNNING IT
; ======================================================================
;
;  PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4V3dFbClear.pi4 -t pi4 \
;      --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;      -o _work/v3dfbclear.img
;  stage _work/v3dfbclear.img at 0x400000 with tools/pi4_upload.py, run
;  "screen on", then enter it at 0x400000.
;
;  "screen on" first, so the monitor's own console proves the surface
;  was live BEFORE this program touched it. Without it this program
;  brings the display up itself and the screen goes from black to the
;  clear colour, which is a weaker before-and-after.
; ======================================================================

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
; NO DMA ENGINE IN THIS BUILD, and that is deliberate. display.pi4's
; accelerated fills would make the poison pass faster and would put a
; second bus master on the same surface in the same run. The one thing
; this program has to be able to say is WHICH ENGINE WROTE THE PIXELS,
; so the only writers here are the A72 and the V3D.
#DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3d.pi4"

#FBC_UART_DR = $FE201000
#FBC_UART_FR = $FE201018

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE MEMORY MAP. Everything below the framebuffer is fixed; the
;  framebuffer's address and the page table's SIZE come from the
;  firmware at run time and are printed before anything is built from
;  them.
;
;  The page table is first and is given two megabytes of room, which
;  covers a virtual address space of two gigabytes. display.pi4 :391
;  records that the firmware's framebuffer is converted into the LOW
;  GIGABYTE, so one megabyte is always enough and the second is there
;  so that a board that disagrees produces a refusal with a number in
;  it rather than a table that runs off its own end.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#FB_PT          = $01000000
#FB_PT_MAX      = $00200000
#FB_ILLEGAL     = $01200000
#FB_BCL         = $01201000
#FB_TILELIST    = $01202000
#FB_RCL         = $01204000
#FB_TSTATE      = $01210000
#FB_TALLOC      = $01250000
#FB_OVERFLOW    = $01350000
#FB_GUARD       = $01390000

#FB_BCL_BYTES   = 4096
#FB_TL_BYTES    = 8192
#FB_RCL_BYTES   = 16384
; 510 tiles x 256 bytes is 130,560. 256 KiB is room for 1024 tiles,
; which is 2048 x 2048 - past anything HDMI on this part will produce.
#FB_TSTATE_BYTES = $00040000
; 510 tiles: align(510*64, 4096) + 8192 + 512K = 569,344. A megabyte
; is room for the same 1024 tiles.
#FB_TALLOC_BYTES = $00100000
#FB_OVERFLOW_BYTES = 262144
#FB_GUARD_BYTES = 4096

; The first virtual address above everything this program allocates
; for itself, so the page table is never smaller than its own map even
; if the firmware puts the framebuffer somewhere low.
#FB_OWN_TOP     = $01391000

#FB_PAGE        = 4096

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE COLOURS.
;
;  #FB_CLEAR is the 32-bit word that must end up in every pixel. It is
;  written into TILE_RENDERING_MODE_CFG_CLEAR_COLORS_PART1 unchanged
;  and, with R/B swap off, arrives in memory unchanged - which is what
;  pi4V3dClear.pi4 measured on silicon: clear $44332211 in, 4096 words
;  of $44332211 out.
;
;  IT IS ALSO DisplayRGB(12, 20, 92). display.pi4 :2467 packs 32bpp as
;  $FF << 24 | r << 16 | g << 8 | b, so the byte at the lowest address
;  is BLUE. The GPU's RGBA8 output writes RED at the lowest address.
;  THE TWO DISAGREE, and this program does not paper over it with the
;  store packet's R/B swap bit: it writes the word it wants, counts
;  the word it wants, and PRINTS whether that word is the one
;  display.pi4 would have written for the same colour. A swap bit set
;  to make a picture look right is a swap bit nobody has measured.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#FB_CLEAR   = $FF0C145C
#FB_POISON  = $A5A5A5A5

; 1.0f into ZS_CLEAR_VALUES. There is no depth buffer; the packet is
; emitted because it TERMINATES the rendering-mode configuration.
#FB_ZCLEAR  = $3F800000

; 100 ms for the bin, one second for the render. The render writes
; every pixel of a 1080p surface and is the first job in this tree
; that has real work to do; the kernel's own order of magnitude for a
; V3D wait is 100 ms and this is ten of them, so a timeout means
; stopped rather than slow.
#FB_BIN_WAIT_US    = 100000
#FB_RENDER_WAIT_US = 1000000

; ======================================================================
;  PRINTING - the same shape every V3D probe in this directory uses.
; ======================================================================

Procedure PutC(c.i)
  Define f.i
  Repeat
    f = PeekN(#FBC_UART_FR)
  Until (f & 32) = 0
  PokeL(#FBC_UART_DR, c)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutS(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekA(*s + i)
    If c = 0 : Break : EndIf
    PutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure PutNl()
  PutC(13) : PutC(10)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      PutC(48 + n)
    Else
      PutC(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure PutDec(v.i)
  Define d.i
  Define started.i
  Define digit.i
  If v < 0
    PutC(45)
    v = 0 - v
  EndIf
  If v = 0
    PutC(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  d = 1000000000000000000
  started = 0
  While d > 0
    digit = (v / d) % 10
    If digit <> 0 Or started = 1
      PutC(48 + digit)
      started = 1
    EndIf
    d = d / 10
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure PutPad(*name, width.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekA(*name + i)
    If c = 0 : Break : EndIf
    PutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
  While i < width
    PutC(32)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure RowHex(*name, v.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutPad(*name, 30)
  PutS("$")
  PutHex(v, 8)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure RowDec(*name, v.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutPad(*name, 30)
  PutDec(v)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure PutErr(e.i)
  Select e
    Case #V3D_OK
      PutS("OK")
    Case #V3D_ERR_NOINIT
      PutS("NOINIT - V3dInit() has not passed")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_OFF
      PutS("MMU_OFF - no V3D page table")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_FAULT
      PutS("MMU_FAULT - a page walk failed. See VIO_ADDR.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_ALIGN
      PutS("MMU_ALIGN - an address is not aligned enough")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_RANGE
      PutS("MMU_RANGE - outside the page table, or page 0")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_FLUSH
      PutS("MMU_FLUSH - the TLB clear never finished")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_ABSENT
      PutS("MMU_ABSENT - HUB_IDENT2 says there is no MMU")
    Case #V3D_ERR_CACHE_TIMEOUT
      PutS("CACHE_TIMEOUT - the L2T clean never finished")
    Case #V3D_ERR_CL_ARGS
      PutS("CL_ARGS - a field or a buffer will not do")
    Case #V3D_ERR_CL_FULL
      PutS("CL_FULL - the list ran out of room")
    Case #V3D_ERR_CL_NOBUF
      PutS("CL_NOBUF - a packet with no list open")
    Case #V3D_ERR_BIN_TIMEOUT
      PutS("BIN_TIMEOUT - CLE_BFC never moved")
    Case #V3D_ERR_BIN_OOM
      PutS("BIN_OOM - the tile allocation pool ran out")
    Case #V3D_ERR_RENDER_TIMEOUT
      PutS("RENDER_TIMEOUT - CLE_RFC never moved")
    Case #V3D_ERR_JOB_BUSY
      PutS("JOB_BUSY - the render was offered before the bin finished")
    Default
      PutS("code ")
      PutDec(e)
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

Procedure DumpBytes(base.i, len.i)
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < len
    If (i % 16) = 0
      PutS("  ")
      PutHex(base + i, 8)
      PutS(":")
    EndIf
    PutS(" ")
    PutHex(PeekA(base + i), 2)
    i = i + 1
    If (i % 16) = 0
      PutNl()
    EndIf
  Wend
  If (len % 16) <> 0
    PutNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE SURFACE, AS THE FIRMWARE DESCRIBED IT
;
;  NOTHING HERE IS A CONSTANT. anvil.pi4:5222-5225 records that this
;  firmware applies overscan of its own accord and has been seen to
;  answer 1824 x 984 to a request for 1920 x 1080. A probe with 1920
;  compiled into it would then render 1920 pixels into a 1824-pixel
;  row and the picture would shear - which looks like a tile
;  arithmetic bug and is not one.
; ======================================================================

Global fbBase.i    = 0
Global fbW.i       = 0
Global fbH.i       = 0
Global fbPitch.i   = 0
Global fbSize.i    = 0
Global fbUsed.i    = 0
Global fbPage0.i   = 0
Global fbMapEnd.i  = 0
Global fbMapBytes.i = 0
Global fbTail.i    = 0
Global fbTailBytes.i = 0

Global ptBytes.i   = 0
Global ptEntries.i = 0

; ======================================================================
;  THE COUNTERS
; ======================================================================

Global nCorrect.i     = 0
Global nPoison.i      = 0
Global nOther.i       = 0
Global firstOtherX.i  = -1
Global firstOtherY.i  = -1
Global firstOtherVal.i = 0
Global tilesTotal.i   = 0
Global tilesFull.i    = 0
Global tilesEmpty.i   = 0
Global firstBadTx.i   = -1
Global firstBadTy.i   = -1
Global guardATouched.i = 0
Global guardBTouched.i = 0
Global usPoison.i     = 0
Global usVerify.i     = 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Verify - every pixel of the surface, grouped by tile.
;
;  THE LOOP IS TILE-MAJOR AND THAT IS THE POINT. A pixel-major loop
;  produces one number for the whole frame; this one produces a
;  verdict per tile, so "the sweep only visited the first supertile"
;  and "the store address was wrong" are different answers instead of
;  the same low percentage.
;
;  The right and bottom tiles are PARTIAL - 1920 is 30 whole tiles but
;  1080 is 16 whole tiles and 56 rows - so the inner bounds are
;  clipped to the surface. Those partial tiles are the ones a 64 x 64
;  test could not have exercised at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Verify(tileW.i, tileH.i, tilesX.i, tilesY.i)
  Define tx.i
  Define ty.i
  Define x0.i
  Define y0.i
  Define x1.i
  Define y1.i
  Define x.i
  Define y.i
  Define v.i
  Define bad.i
  Define good.i
  Define rowBase.i
  Define t0.i

  t0 = Micros()
  tilesTotal = tilesX * tilesY
  ty = 0
  While ty < tilesY
    tx = 0
    While tx < tilesX
      x0 = tx * tileW
      y0 = ty * tileH
      x1 = x0 + tileW
      y1 = y0 + tileH
      If x1 > fbW : x1 = fbW : EndIf
      If y1 > fbH : y1 = fbH : EndIf
      bad  = 0
      good = 0
      y = y0
      While y < y1
        rowBase = fbBase + (y * fbPitch)
        x = x0
        While x < x1
          v = PeekN(rowBase + (x * 4))
          If v = #FB_CLEAR
            good = good + 1
            nCorrect = nCorrect + 1
          Else
            bad = bad + 1
            If v = #FB_POISON
              nPoison = nPoison + 1
            Else
              nOther = nOther + 1
              If firstOtherX < 0
                firstOtherX = x
                firstOtherY = y
                firstOtherVal = v
              EndIf
            EndIf
          EndIf
          x = x + 1
        Wend
        y = y + 1
      Wend
      If bad = 0
        tilesFull = tilesFull + 1
      Else
        If good = 0
          tilesEmpty = tilesEmpty + 1
        EndIf
        If firstBadTx < 0
          firstBadTx = tx
          firstBadTy = ty
        EndIf
      EndIf
      tx = tx + 1
    Wend
    ty = ty + 1
  Wend
  usVerify = Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The two guards, read back word by word.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure VerifyGuards()
  Define i.i
  Define n.i

  i = 0
  n = #FB_GUARD_BYTES / 4
  While i < n
    If PeekN(#FB_GUARD + (i * 4)) <> #FB_POISON
      guardATouched = guardATouched + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  i = 0
  n = fbTailBytes / 4
  While i < n
    If PeekN(fbTail + (i * 4)) <> #FB_POISON
      guardBTouched = guardBTouched + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  MAIN
; ======================================================================

Procedure Main()
  Define r.i
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define t0.i
  Define ok.i
  Define rgbWord.i

  PutNl()
  PutS("=== Pi 4 V3D stage 5a - the GPU clears the HDMI surface ===")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  0. Stage 0. The CLE is a bus master and V3D 7.1 puts different
  ;     registers at these offsets.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = V3dInit()
  PutS("0   stage 0: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  RowHex("HUB_IDENT1", V3dIdent1())
  RowHex("MMU_DEBUG_INFO", V3dMmuDebug())
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: STAGE0")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  If TimerInit() = 0
    PutS("!! the architectural counter is not running - times will read 0")
    PutNl()
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  1. THE SURFACE. Asked for, not assumed.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("1   the HDMI surface")
  PutNl()
  If MailboxInit() = 0
    PutS("    mailbox: REFUSED")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: NO SURFACE")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DisplayStandardTags() = 0
    PutS("    display tags: REFUSED")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: NO SURFACE")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DisplayInit(1920, 1080, 32) <> 1
    PutS("    DisplayInit: rc ")
    PutDec(DisplayLastError())
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: NO SURFACE")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  fbBase  = DisplayBase()
  fbW     = DisplayWidth()
  fbH     = DisplayHeight()
  fbPitch = DisplayPitch()
  fbSize  = DisplaySize()
  fbUsed  = fbPitch * fbH

  RowDec("width", fbW)
  RowDec("height", fbH)
  RowDec("pitch, bytes", fbPitch)
  RowDec("bytes per pixel", DisplayBytesPerPixel())
  RowHex("base, ARM physical", fbBase)
  RowHex("base, VideoCore bus", DisplayBusBase())
  RowDec("allocation, bytes", fbSize)
  RowDec("pitch * height", fbUsed)
  PutNl()

  If DisplayBytesPerPixel() <> 4
    PutS("    this program is 32bpp only and the firmware gave ")
    PutDec(DisplayBytesPerPixel() * 8)
    PutS(" bits")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: NOT 32BPP")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If fbUsed > fbSize
    PutS("    pitch * height is larger than the allocation - refusing")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: SURFACE")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The framebuffer's alignment is the firmware's business: the
  ; allocate tag asks for $100 (display.pi4:2233) and nothing promises
  ; a page. The page table is indexed by page, so the MAPPING is
  ; widened to whole pages around the surface while the RENDER TARGET
  ; keeps the exact address the firmware gave.
  fbPage0    = (fbBase / #FB_PAGE) * #FB_PAGE
  fbMapEnd   = (((fbBase + fbUsed) + (#FB_PAGE - 1)) / #FB_PAGE) * #FB_PAGE
  fbMapBytes = fbMapEnd - fbPage0
  RowHex("first page mapped", fbPage0)
  RowHex("one past last mapped", fbMapEnd)
  RowDec("framebuffer pages", fbMapBytes / #FB_PAGE)
  RowDec("base offset in its page", fbBase - fbPage0)

  ; GUARD B - the slack the firmware allocated and the surface does
  ; not use. Capped at two pages: it is a tripwire, not a survey.
  fbTail      = fbBase + fbUsed
  fbTailBytes = fbSize - fbUsed
  If fbTailBytes > 8192
    fbTailBytes = 8192
  EndIf
  fbTailBytes = (fbTailBytes / 4) * 4
  RowHex("guard B, address", fbTail)
  RowDec("guard B, bytes", fbTailBytes)
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  2. THE PAGE TABLE'S SIZE, DERIVED. Four bytes per 4 KiB of
  ;     virtual address space, and the highest virtual address this
  ;     program uses is the end of the framebuffer.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  n = fbMapEnd
  If n < #FB_OWN_TOP
    n = #FB_OWN_TOP
  EndIf
  ptEntries = n / #FB_PAGE
  ptBytes   = (((ptEntries * 4) + (#FB_PAGE - 1)) / #FB_PAGE) * #FB_PAGE

  PutS("2   the page table")
  PutNl()
  RowHex("table at", #FB_PT)
  RowDec("entries needed", ptEntries)
  RowDec("table bytes", ptBytes)
  RowDec("table bytes reserved", #FB_PT_MAX)
  If ptBytes > #FB_PT_MAX
    PutS("    the framebuffer is too high for the reserved table")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: PAGE TABLE TOO SMALL")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The table must not sit inside anything it maps.
  If (#FB_PT + ptBytes) > #FB_ILLEGAL
    PutS("    the table would overrun the illegal page")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: LAYOUT")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If fbPage0 < #FB_OWN_TOP
    PutS("    the firmware put the framebuffer inside this program's own map")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: LAYOUT")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  3. THE FRAME. Everything the hardware derives from the surface
  ;     size, printed before anything is built from it.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = V3dRenderBegin(fbW, fbH, #V3D_OFMT_RGBA8)
  PutS("3   the frame: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: GEOMETRY")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  RowDec("tile width", V3dTileW())
  RowDec("tile height", V3dTileH())
  RowDec("tiles across", V3dTilesX())
  RowDec("tiles down", V3dTilesY())
  RowDec("tiles total", V3dTilesX() * V3dTilesY())
  RowDec("supertile width, tiles", V3dSupertileW())
  RowDec("supertile height, tiles", V3dSupertileH())
  RowDec("frame width, supertiles", V3dFrameStW())
  RowDec("frame height, supertiles", V3dFrameStH())
  RowDec("tile alloc needed", V3dTileAllocNeeded())
  RowDec("tile alloc given", #FB_TALLOC_BYTES)
  RowDec("tile state needed", V3dTileStateNeeded())
  RowDec("tile state given", #FB_TSTATE_BYTES)
  PutNl()

  ; THIS PROGRAM EXISTS TO EXERCISE MORE THAN ONE TILE. If the surface
  ; turned out to be one tile it would be pi4V3dClear.pi4 again with a
  ; bigger page table, and a pass would mean nothing it does not
  ; already mean.
  If (V3dTilesX() * V3dTilesY()) < 2
    PutS("    one tile only - this probe has nothing to prove here")
    PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: NOT MULTI-TILE")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  r = V3dRenderTarget(fbBase, fbUsed, #V3D_MEMFMT_RASTER, fbPitch, 0)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("    the render target: ") : PutErr(r) : PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: TARGET")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  V3dRenderClear(#FB_CLEAR, #FB_ZCLEAR, 0)
  r = V3dRenderPool(#FB_TALLOC, #FB_TALLOC_BYTES, #FB_TSTATE, #FB_TSTATE_BYTES)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("    the pools: ") : PutErr(r) : PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: POOLS")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  r = V3dBinOverflow(#FB_OVERFLOW, #FB_OVERFLOW_BYTES)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("    the overflow pool: ") : PutErr(r) : PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: POOLS")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  4. POISON. The whole surface, both guards, and the first page of
  ;     each pool.
  ;
  ;     THE SCREEN GOES GREY HERE and that is the before-and-after
  ;     picture: $A5A5A5A5 is a flat mid-grey, so a human watching the
  ;     monitor sees the console vanish under grey and then the grey
  ;     replaced by the clear colour. Neither step is evidence - the
  ;     counts below are - but a run that leaves grey on the screen
  ;     has told the room the CPU wrote and the GPU did not.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("4   poisoning ")
  PutDec(fbUsed)
  PutS(" bytes of surface")
  PutNl()
  t0 = Micros()
  i = 0
  n = fbUsed / 4
  While i < n
    PokeN(fbBase + (i * 4), #FB_POISON)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  usPoison = Micros() - t0
  RowDec("microseconds", usPoison)

  i = 0
  n = #FB_GUARD_BYTES / 4
  While i < n
    PokeN(#FB_GUARD + (i * 4), #FB_POISON)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  i = 0
  n = fbTailBytes / 4
  While i < n
    PokeN(fbTail + (i * 4), #FB_POISON)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  i = 0
  n = 4096 / 4
  While i < n
    PokeN(#FB_TALLOC + (i * 4), #FB_POISON)
    PokeN(#FB_TSTATE + (i * 4), #FB_POISON)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  5. THE V3D MMU.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("5   the V3D MMU: ")
  t0 = Micros()
  r = V3dMmuInit(#FB_PT, ptBytes, #FB_ILLEGAL)
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: MMU")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  r = V3dMmuMap(#FB_BCL, #FB_BCL, #FB_BCL_BYTES)
  If r = #V3D_OK
    r = V3dMmuMap(#FB_TILELIST, #FB_TILELIST, #FB_TL_BYTES)
  EndIf
  If r = #V3D_OK
    r = V3dMmuMap(#FB_RCL, #FB_RCL, #FB_RCL_BYTES)
  EndIf
  If r = #V3D_OK
    r = V3dMmuMap(#FB_TSTATE, #FB_TSTATE, #FB_TSTATE_BYTES)
  EndIf
  If r = #V3D_OK
    r = V3dMmuMap(#FB_TALLOC, #FB_TALLOC, #FB_TALLOC_BYTES)
  EndIf
  If r = #V3D_OK
    r = V3dMmuMap(#FB_OVERFLOW, #FB_OVERFLOW, #FB_OVERFLOW_BYTES)
  EndIf
  If r = #V3D_OK
    r = V3dMmuMap(fbPage0, fbPage0, fbMapBytes)
  EndIf
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("    mapping: ") : PutErr(r) : PutNl()
    PutS("verdict: MMU")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  RowDec("microseconds", Micros() - t0)
  RowDec("pages mapped", V3dMmuPagesMapped())
  RowHex("PTE for the framebuffer", V3dMmuEntry(fbPage0))
  RowHex("PTE, last surface page", V3dMmuEntry(fbMapEnd - #FB_PAGE))
  RowHex("PTE, one page past it", V3dMmuEntry(fbMapEnd))
  RowHex("PTE for guard A", V3dMmuEntry(#FB_GUARD))
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  6. THE BIN LIST. A clear bins nothing: the prologue and the
  ;     epilogue, six packets, and no primitive - which is exactly
  ;     what Mesa submits for glClear (v3dx_draw.c:1793-1806). The
  ;     binner still has to RUN, because the render list branches into
  ;     a per-tile list only the PTB produces.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = V3dBclBegin(#FB_BCL, #FB_BCL_BYTES)
  If r = #V3D_OK
    r = V3dBclFinish()
  EndIf
  PutS("6   the bin list: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: BCL")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  RowDec("bin list bytes", V3dBclBytes())
  DumpBytes(V3dBclStartAddr(), V3dBclBytes())
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  7. THE RENDER LIST. THIS IS THE PART THAT HAS NEVER BEEN
  ;     EXERCISED: the supertile sweep emits one SUPERTILE_COORDINATES
  ;     packet per supertile, and until now the frame has had one.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = V3dRclBuild(#FB_TILELIST, #FB_TL_BYTES, #FB_RCL, #FB_RCL_BYTES)
  PutS("7   the render list: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: RCL")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  RowDec("generic tile list bytes", V3dTileListBytes())
  DumpBytes(V3dTileListStartAddr(), V3dTileListBytes())
  RowDec("render list bytes", V3dRclBytes())
  RowDec("supertile packets expected", V3dFrameStW() * V3dFrameStH())
  ; The head of the list, which is the configuration, and then the
  ; first sixteen bytes of the sweep. The sweep itself is hundreds of
  ; three-byte packets and printing all of it would cost more serial
  ; time than the render costs GPU time; the gate decodes it in full
  ; out of memory, which is where a decoder should read it from.
  PutS("    render list, first 128 bytes:")
  PutNl()
  DumpBytes(V3dRclStartAddr(), 128)
  PutS("    render list, last 32 bytes:")
  PutNl()
  DumpBytes(V3dRclEndAddr() - 32, 32)
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  8. THE BIN JOB.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = V3dBinSubmit()
  PutS("8   bin submit: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: BINSUBMIT")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  r = V3dBinWait(#FB_BIN_WAIT_US)
  PutS("    bin wait: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  RowHex("CLE_BFC before", V3dBinBfcBefore())
  RowHex("CLE_BFC after", V3dBinBfcAfter())
  RowHex("CLE_CT0CA", V3dBinCt0ca())
  RowHex("CLE_CT0EA", V3dBinCt0ea())
  RowHex("PTB_BPCA", V3dBinBpca())
  RowHex("PTB_BPCS", V3dBinBpcs())
  RowHex("CTL_INT_STS", V3dBinIntSts())
  RowHex("ERR_STAT", V3dBinErrStat())
  RowHex("MMU_CTL", V3dBinMmuCtl())
  RowHex("MMU_VIO_ADDR", V3dBinVioAddr())
  RowDec("bin spins", V3dBinSpins())
  RowDec("OOM events", V3dBinOomCount())
  PutS("    the tile allocation pool, first 32 bytes:")
  PutNl()
  DumpBytes(#FB_TALLOC, 32)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: BIN")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  9. THE RENDER JOB. 510 tiles, each cleared and stored.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = V3dRenderSubmit()
  PutS("9   render submit: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("verdict: RENDERSUBMIT")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  t0 = Micros()
  r = V3dRenderWait(#FB_RENDER_WAIT_US)
  PutS("    render wait: ")
  PutErr(r)
  PutNl()
  RowDec("microseconds", Micros() - t0)
  RowHex("CLE_RFC before", V3dRenderRfcBefore())
  RowHex("CLE_RFC after", V3dRenderRfcAfter())
  RowHex("CLE_CT1CA", V3dRenderCt1ca())
  RowHex("CLE_CT1EA", V3dRenderCt1ea())
  RowHex("CTL_INT_STS", V3dRenderIntSts())
  RowHex("ERR_STAT", V3dRenderErrStat())
  RowHex("MMU_CTL", V3dRenderMmuCtl())
  RowHex("MMU_VIO_ADDR", V3dRenderVioAddr())
  RowDec("render spins", V3dRenderSpins())
  RowDec("cache clean result", V3dRenderCleanResult())
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  10. THE PIXELS. This is the whole test.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Verify(V3dTileW(), V3dTileH(), V3dTilesX(), V3dTilesY())
  VerifyGuards()

  rgbWord = DisplayRGB(12, 20, 92)

  PutS("10  the pixels")
  PutNl()
  RowDec("pixels total", fbW * fbH)
  RowHex("clear colour", #FB_CLEAR)
  RowHex("DisplayRGB(12,20,92)", rgbWord)
  RowDec("pixels correct", nCorrect)
  RowDec("pixels still poison", nPoison)
  RowDec("pixels some other value", nOther)
  RowDec("first other, x", firstOtherX)
  RowDec("first other, y", firstOtherY)
  RowHex("first other, value", firstOtherVal)
  RowDec("verify microseconds", usVerify)
  PutNl()
  RowDec("tiles total", tilesTotal)
  RowDec("tiles fully correct", tilesFull)
  RowDec("tiles with nothing right", tilesEmpty)
  RowDec("first wrong tile, column", firstBadTx)
  RowDec("first wrong tile, row", firstBadTy)
  RowDec("guard A words touched", guardATouched)
  RowDec("guard B words touched", guardBTouched)
  PutNl()

  ; The channel-order reading, stated rather than corrected. See THE
  ; COLOURS above.
  If rgbWord = #FB_CLEAR
    PutS("    channel order: the GPU's RGBA8 store and display.pi4 agree")
  Else
    PutS("    channel order: they DISAGREE - the surface is not R-first")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE VERDICT. Every tile right, nothing left over, both guards
  ;  clean, and more than one tile - the last because a pass on a
  ;  single tile is pi4V3dClear.pi4's pass and not this one's.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ok = 1
  If tilesTotal < 2 : ok = 0 : EndIf
  If tilesFull <> tilesTotal : ok = 0 : EndIf
  If nPoison <> 0 : ok = 0 : EndIf
  If nOther <> 0 : ok = 0 : EndIf
  If nCorrect <> (fbW * fbH) : ok = 0 : EndIf
  If guardATouched <> 0 : ok = 0 : EndIf
  If guardBTouched <> 0 : ok = 0 : EndIf

  If ok = 1
    PutS("verdict: OK - the GPU cleared ")
    PutDec(tilesTotal)
    PutS(" tiles of the HDMI surface")
  Else
    PutS("verdict: NOT CLEARED")
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ProcedureReturn nCorrect
EndProcedure
